from itertools import product

def is_tautology(f, n):
    return all(f(*row) for row in product([True, False], repeat=n))

def equivalent(f, g, n):
    return is_tautology(lambda *row: bool(f(*row)) == bool(g(*row)), n)
```

Because a formula in $n$ variables has only $2^n$ rows, this check is a complete proof of a propositional law — but the number of rows doubles with every new variable.
